---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import CollisionsSim from '../../simulators/collisions/CollisionsSim.astro';
import { collisionsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/collisions';

const toc = [
  { id: 'momentum', label: 'What is momentum?' },
  { id: 'impulse', label: 'Impulse' },
  { id: 'conservation', label: 'Conservation of momentum' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Kinetic energy in collisions' },
  { id: 'types', label: 'Elastic, inelastic and sticky' },
  { id: 'two-d', label: 'Collisions in two dimensions' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model assumes' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Chapter 9: Linear Momentum and Collisions. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-introduction' },
  { text: 'Urone, P. P., & Hinrichs, R. (2020). College Physics 2e, Chapter 8: Linear Momentum and Collisions. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/8-introduction-to-linear-momentum-and-collisions' },
  { text: 'Halliday, D., Resnick, R., & Walker, J. (2018). Fundamentals of Physics (11th ed.), Chapter 9: Center of Mass and Linear Momentum. Wiley.' },
  { text: 'Taylor, J. R. (2005). Classical Mechanics, Chapter 3: Momentum and Angular Momentum. University Science Books.' },
  { text: 'Hewitt, P. G. (2015). Conceptual Physics (12th ed.), Chapter 6: Momentum. Pearson.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="physics"
  topic="momentum-collisions"
  description="Interactive momentum and collisions simulator: aim two pucks on an air table, choose elastic, inelastic or perfectly inelastic collisions, and compare momentum and kinetic energy before and after."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={collisionsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A snooker ball stops dead and the ball it hits rolls on. Two cars crumple and slide off together. A tiny bullet sets a
      heavy block moving. Every collision follows the same rule: the total momentum does not change. Aim two pucks on a
      frictionless air table, choose how bouncy the collision is, and compare what is conserved and what is not.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <CollisionsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Drag the <strong>pucks</strong> to place them, and drag the tip of each <strong>velocity arrow</strong> to aim it. Or choose a puck under <strong>Edit puck</strong> and use the sliders.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Elastic</strong>, <strong>Inelastic</strong> (set e) or <strong>Sticky</strong>, then press <strong>Start</strong>.</li>
      <li>Compare the momentum diagrams and the kinetic energy bars before and after. Watch the green centre of mass glide straight through the collision.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Replay</strong> to watch again, or use slow motion. Save runs on the graph to compare collision types.</li>
      <li>Try the scenarios: a still target, head-on, heavy hits light, a glancing blow and two pucks that stick.</li>
      <li>Tap any part of the picture for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> restores every setting.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="momentum">What is momentum?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Momentum</strong> is mass in motion: <strong>p = m v</strong>, where m is the mass in kilograms and v the
    velocity in metres per second. Its unit is kg·m/s. Momentum is a <strong>vector</strong>: it points the same way as the
    velocity. A slow lorry and a fast tennis ball can have the same momentum; a lorry at speed has a great deal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="impulse">Impulse</h2>
  <p>
    To change an object’s momentum you must push on it for some time. The <strong>impulse</strong> is the force multiplied
    by the time it acts: <strong>J = F Δt = Δp</strong>, measured in newton seconds (N·s, the same as kg·m/s). The same
    change in momentum can come from a large force acting briefly or a small force acting for longer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="conservation">Conservation of momentum</h2>
  <p>
    During a collision, A pushes on B and B pushes back on A with an equal and opposite force (Newton’s third law), for
    exactly the same time. So their impulses are equal and opposite, and whatever momentum B gains, A loses. For an
    <strong>isolated system</strong>, one with no outside forces, <strong>the total momentum stays the same</strong>:
  </p>
  <p><strong>m<sub>A</sub>v<sub>A</sub> + m<sub>B</sub>v<sub>B</sub> = m<sub>A</sub>v<sub>A</sub>′ + m<sub>B</sub>v<sub>B</sub>′</strong></p>
  <p>
    The primes mark velocities after the collision. A useful consequence: the <strong>centre of mass</strong> of the system
    moves at a constant velocity, p<sub>total</sub>/M, straight through the collision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="energy">Kinetic energy in collisions</h2>
  <p>
    Kinetic energy, <strong>KE = ½ m v²</strong>, is not a vector and is <em>not</em> always conserved in collisions. Some
    of it can become heat, sound and permanent changes of shape. Energy as a whole is still conserved; it just changes form.
    How bouncy a collision is can be described by the <strong>coefficient of restitution e</strong>: the speed at which the
    objects separate divided by the speed at which they approached.
  </p>

  <h2 id="types">Elastic, inelastic and sticky</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Collision</th><th>Momentum</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Elastic</strong> (e = 1)</td><td>Conserved</td><td>Conserved</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Inelastic</strong> (0 &lt; e &lt; 1)</td><td>Conserved</td><td>Partly lost</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Perfectly inelastic</strong> (stick)</td><td>Conserved</td><td>Most loss possible</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    In a head-on elastic collision between equal masses, the objects swap velocities: the moving one stops and the target
    moves off. When a heavy object hits a light one, the heavy one barely slows and the light one shoots off at up to twice
    the incoming speed. In a perfectly inelastic collision the objects move off together at
    v′ = (m<sub>A</sub>v<sub>A</sub> + m<sub>B</sub>v<sub>B</sub>)/(m<sub>A</sub> + m<sub>B</sub>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-d">Collisions in two dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    Momentum is a vector, so it is conserved separately in each direction: the x-components add up to the same total
    before and after, and so do the y-components. In the momentum diagram the individual momenta are added tip to tail, and
    the resultant is identical before and after. Smooth pucks push on each other only along the line joining their centres
    at the moment of contact. In a glancing elastic collision between equal masses, one of which starts at rest, the two
    move off at right angles to each other, as snooker players know well.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Vehicle safety</strong>: crumple zones, airbags and seatbelts lengthen the collision time so that the same change in momentum produces a smaller force on the people inside.</li>
    <li><strong>Sport</strong>: snooker, bowling and football all rely on momentum transfer; following through keeps the impulse acting for longer.</li>
    <li><strong>Rockets and recoil</strong>: exhaust gases and a gun’s bullet carry momentum one way, so the rocket or gun moves the other.</li>
    <li><strong>Forensic science</strong>: investigators work backwards from skid marks and final positions to the speeds before a crash.</li>
    <li><strong>Particle physics</strong>: conservation of momentum reveals invisible particles in collisions at accelerators.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Momentum is lost in a crash.”</strong> Kinetic energy may be lost, but the total momentum of an isolated system is not.</li>
    <li><strong>“Momentum and kinetic energy are the same thing.”</strong> Momentum is a vector (mv); kinetic energy is a scalar (½mv²).</li>
    <li><strong>“The bigger object pushes harder.”</strong> The forces on the two objects are always equal and opposite; the lighter one simply changes velocity more.</li>
    <li><strong>“Objects that stick together have lost their momentum.”</strong> They share it, and keep moving unless their momenta were equal and opposite.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model assumes</h2>
  <p>
    The pucks slide without friction or air resistance and do not spin. The table has no walls, so the two pucks form an
    isolated system; the simulation stops when they leave it. Each collision is treated as instantaneous, with the impulse
    acting along the line of centres and a constant coefficient of restitution. In the sticky collision the two pucks move
    off together at the centre-of-mass velocity; real objects that stick after an off-centre hit would also start to
    rotate. Puck sizes grow with mass only to make the difference visible.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Momentum p = mv is a vector; impulse J = FΔt equals the change in momentum.</li>
    <li>In an isolated system, total momentum is conserved in every collision.</li>
    <li>Kinetic energy is conserved only in elastic collisions; inelastic collisions turn some of it into other forms.</li>
    <li>Objects that stick together move at v′ = (m<sub>A</sub>v<sub>A</sub> + m<sub>B</sub>v<sub>B</sub>)/(m<sub>A</sub> + m<sub>B</sub>).</li>
    <li>The centre of mass of an isolated system moves at constant velocity, even through a collision.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
